const lectureEntries=[
 {id:"lecture-1",date:"2026.09.03",label:"Class 1",title:"Course frame and group formation",source:"Group Dynamics (1).docx",slides:"Slides 125–191",slideSummary:"Affluent professionals, regional cultures, class and education, technology, and methodological problems.",terms:["culture and ideology","symbolic economy","market segmentation","therapeutic culture"],points:["The course treats American society as overlapping and competing groups shaped by region, class, religion, profession, and education.","Industrial and post-industrial change reorganized work and family roles. College-educated professionals became a distinct symbolic and cultural class.","Consumption can mark group identity. The Starbucks and Dunkin example connects pricing, self-selection, and socioeconomic status.","The therapeutic worldview turns salvation inward: the person seeks a better self through psychological language and professional help."],questions:["How do technologies allow groups to grow beyond face-to-face communities?","When does lifestyle become evidence of class position rather than individual preference?"]},
 {id:"lecture-2",date:"2026.09.08",label:"Class 2",title:"Requirements, class reproduction, and course map",source:"Group Dynamics (2).docx",slides:"Slides 2 and 125–191",slideSummary:"Course units plus the later sections on class, regionalism, technology, higher education, and research reliability.",terms:["reading response","class reproduction","regionalism","replication crisis"],points:["Take-home exams function as short research papers and must connect readings, lectures, discussion, and outside primary research.","Reading responses critically evaluate a selected text and apply it to a real-world experience. The strongest work makes an argument and uses several textual examples.","Household experience and schooling operate as major mechanisms for reproducing social class and privilege.","The course later connects nationalism, regional culture, education, algorithmic capitalism, and the replication crisis in psychology."],questions:["Which course assignments require outside research, and which emphasize close reading?","How do household and school reproduce class while appearing meritocratic?"]},
 {id:"lecture-3",date:"2026.09.10",label:"Class 3",title:"Freud: repression and two models of mind",source:"Group Dynamics (3).docx",slides:"Slides 6–18",slideSummary:"Infantile dependency, repression, the topographic and structural theories, symptoms, and treatment.",terms:["infantile dependency","repression","topographic theory","structural theory","transference"],points:["Freud distinguishes the unconscious from the everyday idea of a subconscious. Repression requires an active force that keeps a wish outside awareness.","In the topographic model, the unconscious follows the pleasure principle while the preconscious censors dangerous wishes. A symptom forms when repression only partly succeeds.","A compromise symptom can redirect unacceptable hostility or desire into obsession, compulsion, anxiety, or another tolerable action.","Freud later replaces the topographic model with id, ego, and superego. This changes pathology from a buried wish into conflict among agencies and changes what treatment aims to do."],questions:["If repression succeeds completely, what evidence could demonstrate that it occurred?","Does insight alone cure a symptom, or does the structural model require a different therapeutic mechanism?"]},
 {id:"lecture-4",date:"2026.09.15",label:"Class 4",title:"Hysteria, hypnosis, and the talking cure",source:"Group Dynamics (4).docx",slides:"Slides 19–48",slideSummary:"Freud's clinical origins, hysteria, Charcot, Anna O., free association, infantile sexuality, and historical influences.",terms:["hysteria","conversion","Charcot","talking cure","free association"],points:["Freud began as a neurologist working with symptoms that had no identifiable physiological explanation, including paralysis, blindness, coughing, and fainting.","French neurology often treated hysteria as constitutional. Freud increasingly explained symptoms through unconscious conflict and conversion.","Hypnosis preceded free association. The talking cure treated speech, memory, and the therapeutic relationship as paths to symptoms.","The problem of childhood sexual memories pushed Freud toward a broader theory of infantile sexuality and toward dream interpretation."],questions:["What is gained and lost when an unexplained bodily symptom becomes evidence of unconscious conflict?","How does the historical meaning of hysteria shape the cases Freud considered clinically important?"]}
];
